# SSR pages, views, HTMX

## A page is one package

Everything a page needs lives in one directory, in `package page`:

```
internal/web/pages/posts/_post_id/
├── page.go      Handler — reads params, calls the usecase, renders
├── usecase.go   Usecase — gathers the data this page shows
├── dto.go       Input (route params), Output (the view model)
└── wire.go      var Set = wire.NewSet(New, NewUsecase)
```

The handler holds `uc *Usecase` directly, with no import, because they are the
same package. Two constructors, distinct names: `New` returns the handler,
`NewUsecase` returns the usecase, so wire sees two different types.

**Render logic deliberately does not live in `internal/usecases/`.** A page
usecase exists to feed one template and has no other caller. Business logic that
is shared, or exposed over the JSON API, belongs in `internal/usecases/` — and a
page is free to call it.

## Paths: three forms of the same route

`gostack g page posts/[post_id]` produces:

| Form | Value | Where it appears |
|---|---|---|
| Page path | `posts/[post_id]` | what you type; Next.js style |
| Filesystem | `posts/_post_id` | the directory — Go import paths reject brackets |
| Gin route | `posts/:post_id` | what gets registered |
| View name | `posts/post_id` | passed to `views.Render` |

The handler reads the parameter by its bare name:

```go
input := Input{PostId: c.Param("post_id")}
```

Rename the directory and the handler stops matching. Prefer regenerating.

## Template inheritance

Go's `html/template` has no parent-template mechanism. Inheritance is done by
parsing the layout, the components and one page into a single template set, then
executing that set starting at the layout.

```html
<!-- views/layouts/base.html -->
{{define "base.html"}}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>{{block "head" .}}{{end}}</head>
<body>
  {{template "navbar.html" .}}
  <main id="main-content">{{block "content" .}}{{end}}</main>
</body>
</html>
{{end}}
```

```html
<!-- views/pages/home.html — only overrides the block -->
{{define "content"}}
  <h1>{{.Message}}</h1>
{{end}}
```

A page file is *only* `define` blocks. Do not add a `{{template "layouts/base.html" .}}`
line — the engine already starts from the layout.

A page template with no `content` block renders blank, silently: executing a
template emits only the text outside its definitions.

## HTMX: the same handler serves both

`views.Engine.Render` branches on the request header:

- no `HX-Request` — executes `base.html`, so the browser gets the full page
- `HX-Request: true` — executes only the `content` block, so HTMX swaps a
  fragment instead of reloading

That is the whole mechanism. One handler, one template, two shapes of response.

```html
<button hx-get="/api/v1/counter" hx-target="#count">+</button>
<span id="count">0</span>
```

For fragments that are not a whole page, render a component directly:

```go
func (h *HTTPv1Handler) Counter(c *gin.Context) {
    out, _ := h.uc.Execute(c.Request.Context(), Input{})
    h.views.RenderPartial(c, "components/counter", out)
}
```

`hx-boost="true"` on a link makes HTMX intercept navigation and swap the target
element — SPA-like routing with no JavaScript router.

## Dev mode

With `VIEWS_DEV_MODE=true` the engine recompiles templates from disk on every
request, so a template edit is visible on refresh without restarting. In
production templates compile once at startup.

## routes.go is generated

`internal/web/routes.go` carries a DO NOT EDIT header and is owned
by `gostack g page`. It returns a marker type `*Pages` rather than `*gin.Engine`,
so wire keeps a single binding for the router while still being forced to build
it — constructing `Pages` is what registers the routes.
